{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"UPDATE — not a pure duplicate; implementing the follow-on fix. The hoopoe agent confirmed (via coordination) that lemuroid-compliant-hoopoe change 2178 (merged as origin/main 60348ef146fa) SCOPES resolve_ref's opening fetch to the requested ref but does NOT short-circuit it: for a bare full SHA it still issues a targeted single-object 'git fetch ... <sha>' want. My distinguishing acceptance criterion — a locally-present full SHA must trigger NO fetch at all — is therefore genuinely uncovered. Retagged our link duplicates -> relates_to.\n\nPROVEN RCA (runtime, /rebar-debug Phase 1):\n- Root cause lineage: attested resolution has fetched-before-resolve unconditionally since the snapshot materialization core (story hoot-glove-foyer, 6d56-8c12-9953-4d25). Originally that opening fetch was an unscoped 'git fetch --no-filter origin' that expanded through remote.origin.fetch to every head (the 512s/300s-timeout field failure). lemuroid-compliant-hoopoe narrowed the blast radius by scoping the refspec, but left a residual: an immutable full SHA already present locally still pays a network round-trip (a scoped want) it does not owe.\n- Confirmed mechanism: with hoopoe's merged code, resolve_ref -> fetch_origin(ref=scoped_fetch_target(sha)) = the sha verbatim as a targeted want. On a --filter=blob:none clone (all commits/trees present), the commit object is already local, so rev_parse resolves it with zero network; the want is pure latency and, on a wedged remote, a needless failure path.\n- Falsifiable RED test proves it: tests/unit/test_snapshot_present_sha_no_fetch.py::test_present_full_sha_issues_no_fetch spies git-fetch argv; pre-fix it records the targeted want (RED), post-fix it records ZERO fetches (GREEN). Verified by reverting the guard: 1 failed (that test) / 3 passed; with the guard: 4 passed.\n\nFIX (Phase 2, minimal, layered on hoopoe's merge):\n- New predicate git_fetch.is_present_full_sha(repo_root, ref): True only for a FULL 40/64-hex object name whose commit is already local. Presence is probed OFFLINE with GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1 (cat-file -e) so a promisor/partial clone does NOT lazily fetch during the check — an absent object returns non-zero and falls through to the explicit targeted-want path.\n- resolve_ref gates its opening fetch: 'if remote_present and not is_present_full_sha(root, ref)'. Moving branches/tags still refresh (scoped); an absent full SHA still takes hoopoe's targeted want and fails closed; blob materialization is unaffected (_ensure_blobs_present does its own targeted --refetch of the SHA).\n\nGUARDRAILS: repo_snapshot.py kept at exactly the 800-LOC cap (predicate lives in git_fetch.py; +1 import offset by tightening an adjacent comment). resolve_ref C901 8->9 (<15, not baselined). make lint, make typecheck, and 512 snapshot/fetch tests (security, partial-clone, timeout, concurrency, plus hoopoe's targeted-fetch tests) all green; test_snapshot_docs green after documenting fetch scope in docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md.\n\ncaused_by note: root origin is hoot-glove-foyer (unconditional fetch-before-resolve); a caused_by link there is blocked because that ticket has no trailer-bearing commit and agents must not --force, so attribution is recorded here in prose."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787558475842572001,"uuid":"ab1013a7-920a-4a3d-9f4f-e592285ebb05"}